        "actor" : {
          "displayName" : "james wolf",
          "id" : "100923804654788847953",
          "image" : {
            "url" : "https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-0GBrvs17dNE/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAA5jM/oKhfIH6ljXg/photo.jpg?sz=50"
          },
          "url" : "https://plus.google.com/100923804654788847953",
          "verification" : {
            "adHocVerified" : "UNKNOWN_VERIFICATION_STATUS"
          }
        },
        "etag" : "\"Q44G99shyMl14lSVN9oTpl4EamI/8_SjCoAv95c2f2rc0fo_6B_xI-4\"",
        "id" : "z131yn3jvov1s3ipp04cexrqywbvcrqgizk0k.1344013676556423",
        "kind" : "plus#comment",
        "object" : {
          "content" : "Hey that&#39;s a really interesting technique, I&#39;ve been doing a lot of proto-board PCB&#39;s like this lately:<br /><a href=\"http://www.flickr.com/photos/23258232@N06/sets/72157629440192292/\" class=\"ot-anchor\">http://www.flickr.com/photos/23258232@N06/sets/72157629440192292/</a>\nI&#39;m trying to keep it neat by setting all the components up in a way that allows for just a single layer of traces on the bottom (notice the lack of wires on the top side).  All of my traces as solder with legs from other components to form the trace lines.  I think of it as the &quot;soviet tank&quot; sort of construction, not pretty and too heavy but almost indestructible.  <br /><br />That example is a netduino mini attached to an xbee for communication.  Works really well in my Headtracking project here:<br /><a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7f4hfq7oAzI\" class=\"ot-anchor\">Headtracking control for Xbox 360 using Netduino&#39;s and a hacked Wiimote</a>",
          "objectType" : "comment"
        },
        "plusoners" : {
          "totalItems" : 0
        },
        "published" : "2012-08-03T17:07:56.556Z",
        "statusForViewer" : {
